============================================================
12. INITIAL LOAD BALANCER TEST
============================================================

Initial test:

Command:

curl.exe http://20.207.206.109


Result:

Connection timed out.


Started troubleshooting layer by layer.


Checked Nginx on VM1.

Command:

az vm run-command invoke `
  -g az-lb-lab-rg `
  -n az-lb-lab-vm1 `
  --command-id RunShellScript `
  --scripts "sudo ss -lntp | grep ':80 '"


Result showed:

LISTEN ... 0.0.0.0:80 ...


Nginx was listening correctly.

The NSGs were then identified as the missing piece because HTTP/80 was not initially allowed.

Added:

allow-http

to both VM NSGs.

Retested the Load Balancer.


============================================================
13. LOAD BALANCER TRAFFIC TEST
============================================================

Command:

1..10 | ForEach-Object {
    curl.exe -s http://20.207.206.109
}


Observed responses from both backend VMs:

Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM1
Hello from VM2
...


Load Balancer traffic distribution was confirmed.


Important observation:

Azure Load Balancer is flow-based rather than a simplistic
request-by-request round-robin mechanism.


============================================================
14. BACKEND FAILURE TEST
============================================================

Stopped Nginx on VM1.

Command:

az vm run-command invoke `
  -g az-lb-lab-rg `
  -n az-lb-lab-vm1 `
  --command-id RunShellScript `
  --scripts "sudo systemctl stop nginx"


VM1 remained in the backend pool.

However, the HTTP health probe began failing.

Tested the Load Balancer repeatedly.

Result:

Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
Hello from VM2
...


VM1 was still configured as a backend but was no longer eligible
for new traffic because it was unhealthy.


